### 14.6 Operational artifact rule

If both a lighter review packet and a stronger operational packet fit:
- keep the ladder visible: `light review memo` -> `stronger packet` -> `operational artifact`
- prefer the stronger packet only when it materially changes auditability or decision quality
- do not emit both as equal-strength outputs for the same control problem
- if operator action is part of the example, show when the lighter memo stays background-only and when the stronger artifact becomes the active verdict surface
- show the sibling relation directly: which lighter review memo precedes the stronger packet, and which operational artifact owns the active verdict surface for that family
- if the example uses benchmark-grade, replay-grade, controller-grade, coding-proof-grade, release-grade, drift-grade, route-quality-grade, or retrieval-substrate-grade language, show the minimum packet floor inside the example rather than leaving it to external lookup
- if a stronger packet supersedes a lighter one, keep the supersession explicit
- if a newer compatible operational artifact exists, supersede the stale predecessor explicitly rather than keeping both live in the same example family
- keep `scenario_id`, `run_id`, `cohort_id`, `trace_id`, and `artifact_version` stable across benchmark, replay, adaptation, release, and telemetry packet families when example structure is used for those artifacts
- before joining operational artifacts inside one exemplar, check precedence, compatibility, freshness, and completeness; reject incompatible merges that would weaken a stronger artifact
- preserve upstream source IDs and `artifact_version` in any joined operational artifact exemplar
